<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Here Is a Map of the Town.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Interpret charts, graphs, and diagrams. Reading: Summarize sections of texts in written form. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of basic syllabification rules when decoding two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret a diagram showing the layout of the town by extracting key information from a text (e.g., "The school is next to the market."). Can summarize written instructions about traditional building techniques (e.g., "First, we collect the palm leaf. Second, we dry the leaves."). Can identify syllable division and apply appropriate stress patterns in two-syllable words, three-syllable words, and compound nouns (e.g., "monument," "streetlamp," "tradition").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large, simple map of a small town square (similar to one used in Lesson 1). Students are asked to recall and identify places they learned previously. Teacher points to different locations and asks, "Where is the library?" "Is the park near the fountain?" using target vocabulary (e.g., <i>library, park, fountain, near, next to, across from</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing locations using "There is/There are" and prepositions of place. For example, pointing to the map, the teacher says, "There is a statue in the town square." "The school is next to the market." The teacher also models the pronunciation of words with /s/ consonant clusters, like "street" and "statue," emphasizing the initial /s/ sound clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If the bank is *across from* the park, are they side by side or facing each other?" (Expected answer: "Facing each other.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'There is a *fountain*', do I mean one fountain or many fountains?" (Expected answer: "One fountain.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a handout with a labeled diagram of a town square, including various buildings (e.g., <i>town hall, library, market, school</i>) and features (e.g., <i>fountain, statue, street, crosswalk</i>). Accompanying the diagram are two short reading texts:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A descriptive paragraph about the town square, using "There is/are" and prepositions of place (e.g., "There is a historic monument in the center. The library is next to the park.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A set of simple instructions on how to build a traditional roof, using imperatives and sequencing words (e.g., "First, gather the palm leaves. Second, dry the leaves in the sun.").</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Collaboratively, in small groups, explain directions and describe map details (e.g., "To get to the park, follow this road up to the intersection.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read both texts silently. The teacher asks: "What is the first text mainly about?" (Expected: The layout of the town square) and "What kind of activity does the second text describe?" (Expected: Building a traditional roof).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching/True/False:</b> Students read the texts again more carefully.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Task 1: Match sentences about locations to specific points on the diagram. For example, "The market is beside the school." Students draw a line or write the number next to the correct building.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Task 2: Read statements about the traditional roof building process and determine if they are True or False based on the text (e.g., "Third, we paint the leaves." - False).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of descriptive and instructional texts. Grammar: Imperatives, sequencing words, There is/are, WH-questions for locations. Vocabulary: <i>town square, map, statue, monument, tradition, market, school, crosswalk, gather, turn, follow, next to, in front of</i>. Phonemic Awareness: Syllable division and stress in two-syllable (e.g., "market"), three-syllable (e.g., "monument," "tradition"), and compound nouns (e.g., "streetlamp," "crosswalk").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Reading:</b> Students receive a new short text describing a route through the town square, but with blanks for prepositions of place and sequencing words. For example: "Walk straight _____ (ahead). The library is _____ (across from) the park. _____ (First), you cross the street." Students fill in the blanks, choosing from a word bank. After completing, students read the text aloud in pairs, paying attention to clear pronunciation of words like "street," "crosswalk." The teacher monitors, providing feedback on reading accuracy and phonemic awareness (e.g., syllable stress in "monument").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Reordering & Syllabification:</b> The teacher provides sentences from the texts (or similar) with words mixed up (e.g., "is / the / library / park / to / next / the"). Students read the scrambled words and reorder them to form correct sentences describing locations. Simultaneously, the teacher presents a list of target vocabulary words (e.g., "monument," "tradition," "streetlamp") and asks students to divide them into syllables and mark the main stress while reading them aloud: "How many syllables in 'monument'? Where is the stress?" The class practices choral repetition for correct stress patterns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading & Peer Check - Follow the Map:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a slightly modified map of the town square with a starting point (e.g., "You are here: at the fountain") and a destination (e.g., "Go to the town hall"). One student reads aloud a short set of written directions (using imperatives and sequencing words: "First, walk straight. Then, turn left at the intersection. Finally, the town hall is on your right.") The other student traces the route on the map, without looking at the directions initially. Then they swap roles and peer-check if the traced route matches the instructions, identifying any misinterpretations from reading.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Summarization Task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a new, slightly more complex diagram of a town square with different labeled buildings and a longer descriptive text about a local "Traditional Town Festival" explaining various activities and where they take place (e.g., "First, visit the craft market near the statue. Then, gather at the fountain for the music. The traditional dance will be across from the town hall."). The text also includes a short section about the steps for a traditional handicraft often made at the festival (e.g., "To make a small basket: First, collect thin branches. Second, weave the branches tightly. Third, dry the finished basket.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Task 1 (Interpreting a Diagram):</b> Students collaboratively read the text about the festival and interpret the diagram. Their task is to answer a set of comprehension questions that require extracting information from both the text and the diagram (e.g., "Where is the traditional dance happening?" "Is the craft market next to the library?" "What is the first step of making a basket?"). They must write their answers in complete sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Task 2 (Summarizing Instructions):</b> Students then collectively summarize the instructions for making the traditional handicraft in 3-4 short sentences, using sequencing words (First, Second, Third) and imperative verbs. They must ensure their summary accurately reflects the steps described in the reading text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, observing reading strategies, collaborative interpretation, and accurate summarization, providing support as needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation of group work using a checklist (for collaboration and participation) and collection of written group responses/summaries for evaluation of reading comprehension and written summarization skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their written answers to the comprehension questions about the "Traditional Town Festival" and their summarized instructions for the traditional handicraft.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in interpreting information from the diagram and descriptive text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and conciseness of the written summary of instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (nouns, prepositions, verbs, adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of "There is/are," imperatives, and sequencing words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of collaborative reading and discussion within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read and understand a map or diagram to find locations." (Yes/Almost/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can summarize short instructions in my own words." (Yes/Almost/No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most challenging part of reading the map or the instructions today? How did I overcome it?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which new words did I find useful for describing places or giving instructions?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading activities allowed students to practice interpreting diagrams and summarizing texts, directly addressing the learning outcomes. The phonemic awareness focus on syllable division helped with decoding new words like "monument" and "tradition." Adjustments needed for next lesson: Perhaps provide more scaffolding for summarizing for some groups. Consider using a digital map for interactive practice. For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), I will ensure students have opportunities to orally describe locations and give directions using the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's focus on reading maps and instructions, and understanding descriptive texts about town places, provides a strong foundation for Lesson 3, where students will actively speak and interact by giving and following directions, and describing their favorite places in the town square.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>